  "editorReview": {
    "text": "This gripping medical history excels in its visceral, scene-by-scene reconstruction of the 1952 polio crisis. The book's great strength lies in chapters like 'The Girl on the Table,' which masterfully translates a complex physiological breakthrough into tangible, high-stakes drama, making the reader feel the desperate rhythm of manual ventilation. The cross-cutting narrative between doctor, student, and machine is brilliantly executed, building relentless tension. However, the momentum falters in later sections, where the immersive, time-stamped discipline gives way to a more conventional, sum",
